{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>If there arise in your midst a prophet or a dreamer of dreams</b> — Scripture calls him <b>a prophet</b> on his own say-so, for he says: HaShem spoke with me while I was awake, and I am His prophet, sent to you that you should do such-and-such. And it is possible that Scripture alludes to the fact that there is truth in this, for in the souls of certain people there is a prophetic power: they know future events through it, the man not knowing whence it comes upon him, but he secludes himself and a spirit comes upon him to say: thus shall be in the future regarding such-and-such a matter. And the philosophers call it \"clairvoyance\" (כהין), and they do not know the cause of the phenomenon, but the matter is verified before the eyes of beholders. Perhaps the soul, in its solitude, cleaves to the Separate Intellect and attunes to it. And this man is called <b>a prophet</b> because he prophesies, and therefore there will come the sign or the wonder that he declares to you." "<b>And he gives you a sign or a wonder</b> — the meaning of \"sign\" (אוֹת): a token concerning a thing that will happen afterward, as in (Numbers 2:2): \"each man by his standard, with signs (בְאֹתֹת).\" For when the prophet comes and says <b>such-and-such a thing is destined to happen</b>, as a portent that it will be so, it is called an אוֹת, as in (Isaiah 7:14): \"Therefore Adonai Himself will give you a sign (אוֹת),\" that El is with us, through the son of the prophet's wife. And the word is derived from (Deuteronomy 33:2) אָתָה. And \"wonder\" (מוֹפֵת) is said of something newly produced before us through a change in the nature of the world, as in (2 Chronicles 32:31): \"to inquire about the wonder (הַמּוֹפֵת) that was done in the land,\" (Joel 3:3): \"And I will give wonders (מוֹפְתִים) in the heavens and on the earth, blood and fire.\" And it is a word shortened from מֻפְלֵאת, like (1 Chronicles 12:38): שֵׁרִית יִשְׂרָאֵל. And language borrows it for something outside the natural order, as stated (Ezekiel 24:24): \"And Ezekiel shall be to you for a wonder (לְמוֹפֵת),\" and it says (Ezekiel 24:27): \"And you shall speak and no longer be silent, and you shall be to them for a wonder (לְמוֹפֵת),\" for his deeds are a marvel (פֶּלֶא) in the eyes of beholders, in the manner of (Lamentations 1:9): \"and she descended marvelously (פְּלָאִים).\" And likewise (Daniel 3:32): \"signs and wonders (אָתַיָּא וְתִמְהַיָּא)... his signs how mighty and his wonders how strong,\" the אֹתוֹת and מוֹפְתִים, for פֶּלֶא and תֵּמַהּ are one matter. And in the Exodus from Egypt there were אֹתוֹת — these are the things He would tell them beforehand, as (Exodus 8:19): \"Tomorrow this shall be the sign (הָאֹת),\" and there were מוֹפְתִים there — things performed as a novelty without prior announcement. And likewise (Isaiah 38:22): \"What is the sign (אוֹת) that I shall go up to the house of HaShem?\" — where He announces it beforehand, and concerning that same sign itself He says (2 Chronicles 32:31): \"to inquire about the wonder (הַמּוֹפֵת) that was done in the land,\" to know how that novelty came about for them. And likewise the staff that turned into a serpent is called (Exodus 4:17) an <b>אוֹת</b> when He made it known through it to the children of Israel, and is called (Exodus 4:21) a <b>מוֹפֵת</b> when He performed it before Pharaoh as a novelty. And likewise (Isaiah 20:3): \"As My servant Isaiah walked naked and barefoot three years, a sign and a wonder (אוֹת וּמוֹפֵת) upon Egypt and upon Cush\" — for there was in the matter an אוֹת indicating what would come, and a marvel (פֶלֶא) in the conduct that the prophet should walk thus. If so, <b>and he gives you a sign or a wonder</b> means: he says, \"I am the prophet sent to you that you should worship such-and-such idolatry, and this is your sign, for tomorrow the locust, the grasshopper, and the gall-worm will come,\" or he gives them a מוֹפֵת — he casts a staff before them and it becomes a serpent.\n\nAnd in the Sifrei they said: an אוֹת is a מוֹפֵת and a מוֹפֵת is an אוֹת, except that the Torah spoke in two expressions. Perhaps their intention is to say that there is no distinction between the two and no difference in their law, and it would have sufficed to mention only one, but the Torah wished to elaborate on the matter, to say: every single אוֹת that he gives you, from among all the אֹתוֹת in the world.\n\nAnd this man who calls himself <b>a prophet</b> and commands the worship of idolatry — by the plain sense, a prophet of idolatry is one who says: \"The image of Pe'or sent me to you, for it is Elohim,\" and commands that you worship it in such-and-such a manner. And this is what Scripture also says of him elsewhere (Deuteronomy 18:20): \"but who speaks in the name of other gods and dies,\" for all who prophesy in the name of other gods, to worship them, prophesy as though they are his gods, and He mentions him there together with false prophets.\n\nBut what emerges from this from the words of our Rabbis (Sanhedrin 90a) is far greater, for this prophet mentioned here prophesies in the name of YHWH: he says, \"The glorious HaShem sent me that you should worship Pe'or, for it was a partner with Him in the work of Creation,\" or \"it is greater than all the gods before Him and He desires that you worship it.\" And they, of blessed memory, stated that even if he says to worship idolatry even for one hour alone — for example, if he says: \"Worship Pe'or today only and you will succeed in such-and-such a matter, for so is the will before the glorious HaShem\" — he is liable to death, and this too is the plain sense of the verse.\n\nAnd behold, Scripture commanded that we not listen to one who prophesies in the name of YHWH to worship idolatry at all, and that we not look at the signs and wonders he performs. And He gave the reason: because we know from the Exodus from Egypt that it was an actual deed, not a vision or an apparition — for to YHWH belongs the earth, and He is the One who renews and desires and is able, and there is no Elohim besides Him. And we know from the standing at Mount Sinai that He, face to face, commanded us to walk in this way — that we not worship another besides Him at all, and that we give His glory to no other. And this is the meaning of (Deuteronomy 13:6): \"which YHWH your Elohim has commanded you.\" And behold: he has spoken falsely (סָרָה) against YHWH — for He never commanded him thus; or he has spoken falsely (סָרָה) against His glory — for it is not fitting to worship another El."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "The transliteration כהין (kehin) is left as Ramban writes it, apparently a medieval Hebrew rendering of a philosophical term for clairvoyance or divination.", 1 "The Aramaic phrases from Daniel are translated in the flow of Ramban's argument rather than left in transliteration, since the point he is making depends on understanding the semantic equivalence between the Aramaic terms and their Hebrew counterparts. The word סָרָה in the closing lines is rendered as 'falsely' following Rashi's understanding of the term as a turn-aside from truth; Ramban may also be playing on its sense of 'rebellion.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:44.232827809Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}